# App
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4100

# Backend (server-side only — never exposed to browser)
BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3100
BACKEND_API_SECRET=dev-backend-api-secret

# Cookie / session signing key. Reserved, not read: the backend seals the session
# cookie and this app only stores what it issues, so `config/env.schema.ts` is its
# only reference today. Local dev default, public in this repository — the schema
# refuses this exact string in staging/production so it cannot later become a live
# key still holding a published value.
# Generate a real one: openssl rand -base64 32
SESSION_SECRET=dev-session-secret-must-be-at-least-32-chars

# Binding token HMAC key — server-only, never exposed to browser. Unlike
# SESSION_SECRET this one is live (lib/auth/binding.ts), so a known value means
# forgeable binding tokens. Same local dev default and the same
# staging/production refusal.
# Generate for production: openssl rand -base64 32
BINDING_SECRET=dev-binding-secret-must-be-at-least-32-chars

# AUTH_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS — the longest a signed-in browser may go without
# touching a member route before it is signed out. Defaults to 28800 (8 hours).
# Drives the binding cookies' maxAge and the expiry inside the binding token
# itself. `proxy.ts` rolls both forward on member requests of every method,
# navigations and server-action POSTs alike, so "idle" means no member traffic at
# all — it just writes when there is something to record rather than on every
# single request (lib/auth/CONTEXT.md).
#
# That makes it a ceiling, not an exact figure: the roll happens once the binding
# is half spent, so the real window runs from half this value to all of it. The
# hourly rotation re-stamps the binding as well, which holds a browser that
# navigates at all near the top of that range.
#
# THIS IS THE SESSION CLOCK YOUR USERS ACTUALLY MEET. The backend's
# AUTH_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS (7 days) is the absolute ceiling on a session's life;
# this is the idle window inside it, and it is the one that fires first. Keep it
# the smaller of the two. Set it larger and nothing breaks — the session cookie
# simply expires first and the visitor lands on /login — but the idle timeout
# then means nothing, and this app cannot read the backend's value to warn you.
#
# Lapsing signs the visitor out rather than prompting to re-authenticate: the
# proxy sends them to /logout, which revokes the session backend-side. Capped at
# 2592000 (30 days), past which this has stopped being an idle timeout and the
# ceiling belongs in AUTH_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS where the server enforces it.
# AUTH_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=28800

# AUTH_ROTATION_RETRY_SECONDS — how long the proxy waits before asking the
# backend to rotate again after a rotation call it could not complete (a
# timeout, a 5xx, a refused connection). Defaults to 60.
#
# Keep it at or below the backend's AUTH_SESSION_ROTATION_GRACE_SECONDS (also 60
# by default, see apps/backend/.env.example). A rotation can commit and still
# lose its answer on the way back here, which leaves the browser holding the
# token that was just retired. Inside the grace window the backend serves that
# token and answers a retry `superseded`, so the proxy asks again on each
# navigation until one lands past the window, where the backend sees no
# successor was ever used and restores the token. A retry set above the window
# leaves a stretch where nothing asks and ordinary requests are refused — a
# forced sign-out before the recovery can run. Neither app can read the other's
# env to check.
#
# The recovery covers a rotation call THIS APP could not complete, and only
# that. Once the answer arrives the proxy spends the new token on its own render,
# so a response lost between here and the browser reads as token reuse instead.
# SECURITY.md has the reasoning.
# AUTH_ROTATION_RETRY_SECONDS=60

# Public — safe to bundle into browser
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=LL Stack

# Logging (server-side) — mirrors the backend's LOG_* knobs.
# LOG_SINK: stdout | http_otlp | seq (default stdout)
LOG_SINK=stdout
# LOG_LEVEL: trace|debug|info|warn|error|fatal (defaults per NODE_ENV)
# LOG_LEVEL=debug
# LOG_REMOTE=true ships server logs through the sink; off (default in dev) prints to terminal.
# LOG_REMOTE=false
# Seq (used when LOG_SINK=seq) — local Seq matches the backend (apps/backend/.env).
# SEQ_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8087
# SEQ_API_KEY=
# LOG_HTTP_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
#
# To view frontend logs in local Seq, set:
#   LOG_REMOTE=true
#   LOG_SINK=seq
#   SEQ_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8087

# Logging (browser) — NEXT_PUBLIC_* is INLINED AT BUILD TIME. Both variables
# below must be set when you run `next build`. Setting them only in the runtime
# environment — build once, configure per environment, the ordinary container
# flow — leaves the shipped bundle on whatever it was built with, and changing
# either one needs a REBUILD. Set them at build time and the boot notice is
# accurate about the bundle: the inlining covers the server compilation too, so
# browserRemoteEnabled on server.client_logs.ingest_disabled is the value this
# app was BUILT with, not merely what the running process was handed. It
# over-reports in one case only — the variable absent at build and set only in
# the runtime environment, where nothing was inlined and the server falls back
# to reading its own env while the bundle stays silent.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LOG_LEVEL=debug
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LOG_REMOTE=true ships browser logs to /api/client-logs; OFF by
# default in every environment. This is the browser half of a two-part switch:
# the server half below (CLIENT_LOG_INGEST_ENABLED) is authoritative — with it
# off, turning this on posts batches into a 404. Enable both to ship browser logs.
# With it off the records are NOT discarded — the browser logger writes `warn`
# and above to the DevTools console instead, in production as in dev, so an error
# boundary still leaves evidence. Below that floor a build with no remote sink
# drops the record, which is what stops a default production build printing to
# every visitor's console on every page load. NEXT_PUBLIC_LOG_LEVEL raises the
# bar from there; it cannot lower it. Two events are left out of that console
# path by name — client.error.unhandled and client.error.rejection — because the
# browser prints those itself; they are still posted when remote is on.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LOG_REMOTE=false

# Browser-log ingest (server side)
# CLIENT_LOG_INGEST_ENABLED is the master switch for POST /api/client-logs — the
# one anonymous, internet-writable endpoint this app exposes. OFF by default
# (the route answers 404, before its rate limit runs), matching the house
# posture for optional infrastructure: OpenTelemetry ships wired up and off
# too. Turn it on deliberately, after reading SECURITY.md's deploy checklist —
# the limits below cap what a hostile caller costs you, but only an edge/WAF
# rule and a sink-side spending quota bound it from outside the app. The boot
# log names this variable (server.client_logs.ingest_disabled) while it is off,
# so missing browser logs are diagnosable server-side (at info while the browser
# half is off too, at warn when NEXT_PUBLIC_LOG_REMOTE=true is posting into that
# 404 — the state that is actually broken).
# CLIENT_LOG_INGEST_ENABLED=true

# CLIENT_LOG_ALLOWED_ORIGIN — leave UNSET unless a proxy in front of Next
# rewrites the Host header. /api/client-logs refuses a request whose Origin does
# not name the host this process received, which is exact while every proxy
# preserves Host and 100% wrong the moment one rewrites it to its upstream (an
# nginx `proxy_pass` without `proxy_set_header Host $host`): every real browser
# request fails the check and all browser telemetry stops, reported once per
# window as server.client_logs.refused with reason=origin_mismatch. Fixing the
# proxy is the better repair. Where the proxy is not yours to change, set this
# to the origin browsers actually address the app on and it REPLACES the Host
# comparison. It is safe where trusting X-Forwarded-Host would not be, because
# you set it and the caller cannot. Scheme, host, and port only — a path or
# query is refused at boot rather than silently ignored.
# Two things it does NOT do. It does not make Origin mandatory: a request that
# sends no Origin header at all is still admitted, here exactly as under the Host
# comparison, so this repairs the comparison rather than tightening it (what
# bounds a non-browser caller is the rate limit and the caps). And it takes ONE
# origin, not a list — an app served on several (apex plus www, per-tenant
# domains) would keep telemetry only from the one named here, so leave it unset
# and fix Host at the proxy instead.
# If refusals continue after you set it, read `originCheck` on the
# server.client_logs.refused record: `allowed_origin` (with the normalised value
# alongside) means this variable is in force and its value is wrong; `host` means
# it is not being read at all.
# CLIENT_LOG_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=https://app.example.com

# Rate-limit / reverse-proxy topology
# TRUST_PROXY tells this app how much of X-Forwarded-For was written by your
# infrastructure rather than by the caller. It is what decides whether the
# /api/client-logs ingest limit buckets per client IP or app-wide, so set it
# DELIBERATELY once you deploy — under-trusting collapses every visitor into one
# bucket. Same variable and meaning as the backend's.
#   unset/false = trust nobody (safe default) | <n> = trust n hops (e.g. 1)
# `true` and Express' named forms (loopback, a subnet) are legal for the backend
# but cannot be honoured here — a Next route handler has no socket address to
# evaluate them against. They resolve to ZERO hops (every caller in one bucket,
# never "trust whatever the caller wrote") and the boot logs
# server.trust_proxy.degraded, so the downgrade is never a surprise. Set a hop
# count if you want per-client buckets on this side of the stack.
#
# A hop count is only as good as your network: it is spoof-resistant while every
# request really does traverse that chain, but a directly reachable port means
# the caller writes the entry the limit keys on. Make sure this app is reachable
# only through the proxies you are declaring.
#
# Count the hops you ACTUALLY run. Over-declaring is not harmless and is not
# caught at boot: entries are counted from the right, so a chain shorter than the
# depth you declare can only fall back to the shared bucket — silently turning
# per-client bucketing off for every request while your config reads as though it
# were on. The app says so at request time instead, once per window, as
# server.trust_proxy.chain_too_short. Above 16 hops the value is treated as a
# typo and degrades to zero like `true` does.
#
# Only X-Forwarded-For is read. If your platform conveys the client address ONLY
# in its own header (CF-Connecting-IP, True-Client-IP, Fly-Client-IP) without
# also appending to X-Forwarded-For, every caller lands in the shared bucket and
# no warning fires — a missing chain looks identical to having no proxy at all.
# TRUST_PROXY=1
#
# FOUR allowances, all per 60s window, in TWO dimensions — and BOTH allowances
# of each dimension are spent on every request: the per-client figure meters the
# caller's own bucket, and the SHARED figure is the whole-app ceiling charged
# alongside it (per-client XOR shared was the earlier design, and it left no
# global ceiling at all once TRUST_PROXY was set — fresh addresses multiplied
# the allowance freely). None of them has an off switch: while ingestion is
# enabled (CLIENT_LOG_INGEST_ENABLED above), the anonymous route is always
# capped.
#
# Dimension 1 — REQUESTS. Charged before the body is read, so a flood is refused
# without parsing anything. This is also what bounds the limiter's memory.
#   CLIENT_LOG_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE — per client, in force once TRUST_PROXY
#                       names a hop count. A bucket is an ADDRESS, not a person:
#                       corporate NAT and mobile CGNAT put hundreds of members
#                       behind one, and a single tab in an error storm flushes
#                       far faster than its idle cadence (20 buffered records
#                       force a flush). An IPv6 bucket is the client's /56
#                       NETWORK, not one address: a v6 subscriber holds a whole
#                       delegated block, so anything finer lets one host mint
#                       buckets freely — and anything this coarse can group v6
#                       neighbours, the same trade NAT already forces on v4.
#                       If you see server.client_logs.throttled
#                       with reason=window_exhausted and sharedBucket:false on
#                       real traffic, raise this. Max 600 — the limiter shrinks
#                       its key map as this rises so its memory stays bounded, so
#                       a bigger allowance buys fewer tracked clients (2800 at
#                       300, 1400 at 600).
#   ..._SHARED_PER_MINUTE — the whole-app request ceiling, charged on EVERY
#                       request. With TRUST_PROXY unset (the default) it is the
#                       only allowance; with it set, it is the global cap a
#                       distributed caller meets once its many addresses add up.
#                       Sized as whole-app: 6000/min is ~500 concurrently active
#                       tabs at the browser logger's idle 5s cadence, or 20
#                       saturated per-client callers. It must be >= the
#                       per-client figure (the schema refuses otherwise). In the
#                       untrusted posture one abuser can spend the whole
#                       allowance — that is what TRUST_PROXY buys you out of. A
#                       shared-bucket throttle record with TRUST_PROXY set means
#                       the GLOBAL ceiling fired: distributed traffic, and this
#                       pair is the lever.
# CLIENT_LOG_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=300
# CLIENT_LOG_RATE_LIMIT_SHARED_PER_MINUTE=6000
#
# Dimension 2 — RECORDS. This is the ingest ceiling proper, and the one your sink
# is actually sized and billed in. Charged once the batch is parsed, because that
# is the first moment its cost is known. A request cap ALONE metered the wrong
# thing: one request may legally carry 100 records, so a caller who packs every
# batch bought roughly a hundred times the ingest of one who sends them singly.
# The unit is byte-aware: a batch charges its record count or its byte size in
# 64KiB/100ths, whichever is larger, so one enormous record costs what its bytes
# carry rather than 1.
# Both defaults are 40x their request counterpart, so each request's parity
# budget is 40 units — 40 records, or ~26 KB of body. In records that is twice
# the browser logger's own 20-record flush threshold; in BYTES it is tighter
# than it sounds, because client.error.* records deliberately carry the minified
# browser stack, so an error-storm batch binds on its bytes well before its
# record count. That is the ceiling doing its job — the sink is billed in
# bytes — but it means record_budget_exhausted is the reason to expect FIRST
# during an error storm, at fewer requests than the request allowance
# advertises. Raise these if you see it on real traffic; unlike the request
# allowance they do NOT shrink the key map, because records are charged onto
# entries that already exist.
# The SHARED record figure is the whole-app ingest ceiling proper — charged on
# every batch, it is what a distributed caller meets, and the app-wide worst
# case your sink can be asked to swallow: 240000 units/min keeps the 40x parity
# (6000 x 40) and is ~150 MB/min in byte-equivalents under active attack. Size
# it against your sink plan, and back it with the provider-side spending quota
# SECURITY.md's checklist calls for — this ceiling is the last in-app line, not
# the only one. Must be >= the per-client figure (the schema refuses otherwise).
# CLIENT_LOG_RECORD_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=12000
# CLIENT_LOG_RECORD_LIMIT_SHARED_PER_MINUTE=240000
#
# The limiter also tracks a bounded number of per-client buckets — 2800 at the
# default request allowance, 1400 at 600, and never more than 10000 however low
# you go. IPv6 clients spend one bucket per /56 network, so a lone delegation
# cannot fill the map by rotating addresses. Past the ceiling a client with no
# bucket is served from the shared whole-app bucket instead of getting its own —
# no live bucket is evicted and no one is refused for the map's fullness alone
# (degraded newcomers do spend the shared allowance, and a refusal they meet
# there carries degraded: true on the throttle record) — and the app says so
# once per window as
# server.client_logs.store_saturated. There is no env knob that raises it, and
# raising CLIENT_LOG_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE makes it WORSE — the map shrinks as
# the request allowance grows. If you see it on real traffic, LOWER that
# allowance (which buys tracked clients back, up to 10000) or wire the shared
# store. The RECORD allowances have no effect here either way. The whole-app
# bucket is exempt from this ceiling — it never consumes a key slot, and a
# caller with no trusted address always has a bucket to land in — though while
# the map is pinned, degraded newcomers share that bucket's allowance. A spray
# big enough to pin the map also has to fit under the shared request ceiling it
# is charged against.
#
# FRONTEND_INSTANCE_COUNT: how many copies of this app you run. Staging and
# production refuse to boot above 1 — the ingest limit counts in-process, so N
# instances means N times the allowance. Mirrors BACKEND_INSTANCE_COUNT.
# This is a DECLARATION, not something the app can observe. On a serverless or
# auto-scaling host (Vercel, Lambda, Cloud Run with min-instances > 1) the
# platform decides the replica count, `1` is simply untrue, and the guard proves
# nothing — wire the shared store before deploying there.
# FRONTEND_INSTANCE_COUNT=1

# DEV_MODE: enables frontend-only local development routes such as /dev.
DEV_MODE=true
